      {
        "id": "CS03-L03",
        "title": "TCP, UDP and connection state",
        "stage": "CS03",
        "domain": "CS03",
        "domainName": "Networking from first principles",
        "route": "Core",
        "hours": 1.4166666666666667,
        "scope": "TCP supplies an ordered byte stream with retransmission and connection state. UDP sends independent datagrams without those guarantees. Applications can add their own reliability over UDP. Ports identify service endpoints, but a port number alone does not establish which application or user is present.\n\nDeeper-study checklist: TCP handshake, retransmission, UDP, QUIC. Use the section references to investigate these terms and connect them to the practice evidence.",
        "practice": "Capture your own local TCP connection and one permitted UDP exchange. Identify setup, payload and teardown or timeout behavior in the trace.",
        "evidenceRequired": "A sanitized explanation and repeatable practical or simulation evidence, with authorization, expected versus actual behavior and limits.",
        "prerequisites": "Build on CS03-L02.",
