      {
        "id": "CS08-L04",
        "title": "Memory-safe design and language tradeoffs",
        "stage": "CS08",
        "domain": "CS08",
        "domainName": "Developer tools and secure coding foundations",
        "route": "Core",
        "hours": 1.4166666666666667,
        "scope": "Memory-safe languages reduce classes of memory misuse but do not remove authorization, business-logic or concurrency defects. Unsafe interfaces and native dependencies can reintroduce memory risks. Choose libraries and abstractions deliberately and review boundary assumptions rather than assuming a language guarantees security.\n\nDeeper-study checklist: C, C++, Rust, Go, Java, JavaScript, memory ownership. Use the section references to investigate these terms and connect them to the practice evidence.",
        "practice": "Compare a safe bounded parser with a deliberately flawed toy parser. Write tests showing the invariant each version does or does not preserve.",
        "evidenceRequired": "A sanitized explanation and repeatable practical or simulation evidence, with authorization, expected versus actual behavior and limits.",
        "prerequisites": "Build on CS08-L03.",
        "requires": [
          "CS08-L03"
        ],
        "resources": [
          "R-GIT",
          "R-SSDF",
          "R-CHEAT"
        ],
        "projects": [
          "CS-P08"
        ],
        "tools": [
          "TOOL-NOTE",
          "TOOL-GIT"
        ],
        "initial": {
          "included": true
        },
        "tasks": [
          {
            "id": "learn",
            "title": "Memory-safe design and language tradeoffs",
            "kind": "Study",
            "minutes": 25,
            "instructions": "Memory-safe languages reduce classes of memory misuse but do not remove authorization, business-logic or concurrency defects. Unsafe interfaces and native dependencies can reintroduce memory risks. Choose libraries and abstractions deliberately and review boundary assumptions rather than assuming a language guarantees security.\n\nDeeper-study checklist: C, C++, Rust, Go, Java, JavaScript, memory ownership. Use the section references to investigate these terms and connect them to the practice evidence.\n\nThink about this: Does memory safety imply application security?",
            "checklist": [],
            "resources": [
              "R-GIT",
              "R-SSDF",
